      "claim": "Peripherally administered BH4 (20 mg/kg i.v.) elevated BH4 in CSF within 90 minutes, demonstrating meaningful CNS access after systemic administration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "In WT mice, i.v. administration of [13C5]-5-formylTHF led to significant brain tissue levels, consistent with transport of labeled folates across the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "Threonine showed unmeasurably low brain uptake by the carotid-injection method, providing a contradictory example among structurally related amino-acid analogs.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Most structurally related evidence molecules show direct, experimentally observed CNS access after systemic administration, including CSF elevations, brain tissue detection, and good CSF penetration. The negative threonine record is low-similarity and contradicted by transporter-mediated brain effects, so the overall prediction is pass.",
